On Thu, May 30, 2013 at 6:12 AM, la arxokuna <gleki.is.my.name@gmail.com>wrote:

> On Thursday, May 30, 2013 3:10:18 PM UTC+4, aionys wrote:
>
>> On Thu, May 30, 2013 at 3:45 AM, la arxokuna <gleki.is...@gmail.com>wrote:
>>
>>> On Thursday, May 30, 2013 11:07:11 AM UTC+4, Pierre Abbat wrote:
>>>>
>>>> On Wednesday, May 29, 2013 22:12:25 la arxokuna wrote:
>>>> > adjectives are NOI-clauses.
>>>> > Adverbs are sei-clauses or fi'o-clauses.
>>>> > Alternatively, both of them are tanru.
>>>>
>>>> A NOI clause is a relative clause.
>>>
>>>
>>> Yes, and relative clauses are adjectives.
>>>
>>
>> No.
>>
>
> A cat which is black = black cat
>

"which is black" is not a adjective. A phrase is not an adjective.


>  I'm not sure what you mean by "fi'o-
>>>> clauses"; I think you mean a prepositional phrase. Neither is an
>>>> adjective or
>>>> adverb;
>>>
>>>
>>> they are adverbs.
>>>
>>
>> No.
>>
>>
>>>  those parts of speech, which are classes of single words, don't exist
>>>> in Lojban.
>>>
>>>
>>> All SAE languages have adverbs. Lojban is no exception.
>>>
>>
>> Lojban is not an SAE language.
>>
>
> Lojban must optionally work as a SAE language. Otherwise it's alien and
> not usable by SAE-speakers.
>

No, Lojban mustn't do anything. And no, it's not unusable by speakers of
SAE languages if it doesn't conform to SAE. Japanese isn't SAE, and yet
there are many English-natives who are perfectly capable of speaking it.


>  There is no syntactic difference between "le mlatu cu blabi" and "le
>>>> blabi cu mlatu", indicating that "mlatu" and "blabi" belong to the same
>>>> part
>>>> of speech.
>>>>
>>>
>>> {le mlatu} is a noun.
>>>
>>
>> *sumti
>>
>>
>>> {mlatu} is a verb.
>>>
>>
>> *selbri
>>
>
>
> The more you replace understandable terms to fenki terms the fewer people
> are willing to learn Lojban.
> Of course it's your choice but please don't try ban other terminologies
> for the same language.
>

The more you use incorrect terms the more you will confuse people and cause
them to make mistakes based on faulty assumptions. {LO broda} is *not* a
noun, it is a sumti. The English equivalent of sumti is "argument". {broda}
is not a verb, it is a selbri: "relationship word".

It's not a matter of choice, it's not a matter of banning, it's a matter of
using an accurate term to describe a part of speech. "noun", "verb",
"adjective", "adverb", etc., are not accurate words to describe *any* part
of Lojban speech, and it's certainly not crazy to use words that are.

